<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tsogt Taij</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1581–1637</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Choghtu Khong Tayiji</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tsogt Taij, known in other sources as Choghtu Khong Tayiji, was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalkha-mongols">Khalkha</a> Mongol prince and poet who supported the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karma-kagyu">Karma Kagyu</a> school against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelug">Gelug</a> in the religious wars of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>. He moved to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qinghai-lake">Kokonor</a> region in the 1630s but was defeated and killed in 1637 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gushi-khan">Güshi Khan</a> of the Khoshut, ally of the Gelug. A rock inscription of his poem survives, and he is a hero of Mongolian literature.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tsogt-taij.jpg" alt="Tsogt Taij" width="320" height="192" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Chinneeb · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tsogtiin_tsagaan_baishin.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
